  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What exactly is SEBI proposing regarding fund switches?",
      "a": "SEBI is proposing that switches from regular plans to direct plans within the *same mutual fund scheme* should be exempt from capital gains tax. This aims to remove the tax disincentive for investors to move to lower-cost direct plans."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does this proposal apply to all types of fund switches?",
      "a": "No, it specifically applies to switches from a regular plan to a direct plan of the *same mutual fund scheme*. It does not cover switches between different schemes or from direct to regular plans."
    },
    {
      "q": "Will I still have to pay exit loads if I switch?",
      "a": "Yes, the proposal primarily addresses capital gains tax. If your fund has an exit load applicable for redemptions before a certain period, and you switch within that period, you may still incur the exit load. Always check your fund's specific terms."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who will benefit most from this change?",
      "a": "Long-term investors who currently hold significant investments in regular plans, especially those with substantial unrealised capital gains, will benefit most. It allows them to transition to direct plans without immediate tax liability, thus saving on costs over time."
    },
    {
      "q": "When is this proposal expected to be implemented?",
      "a": "The proposal is currently under consideration by SEBI and requires approval from the Ministry of Finance and amendments to the Income Tax Act. There is no definitive timeline, but it is a significant regulatory move that could take some time to finalise."
    }
  ],
